course: dictionary
lesson: 15215
title: The word sentiment
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sentiment is a feeling or an opinion about something. When someone says «I share your sentiment», they mean they feel the same way you do. You can tell which meaning fits by reading the words around it.
explain: Sometimes sentiment means tender feelings, such as love, pity or missing the past. «There is no room for sentiment when you choose a team.» Here the sentence tells you that feelings should not get in the way. The clue is the word no, which tells you the writer does not want those feelings.
explain: A sentiment can also be a thought written in words, like the message in a card. «The card had a lovely sentiment inside.» The word inside tells you it is a written message, not a feeling. When you are not sure, swap in a meaning and check the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A sentiment is a feeling or opinion about something.
fact: «I share your sentiment» means I feel the same way as you.
fact: Sentiment can mean tender feelings, such as love, pity or nostalgia, and writers use it to show how characters feel.
fact: In formal writing, a sentiment is a thought expressed in words, like a message in a birthday card.
fact: «Public sentiment» means what many people feel, as in «Public sentiment is against the new road.»
fact: «Sentimental» means full of tender feelings, sometimes too much.
exercise: «The card had a kind sentiment inside: Thinking of you.» What is a sentiment here? | A type of birthday cake | *A thought written in words | A loud noise | A long walk
exercise: «I share your sentiment: the film was far too long.» What does share your sentiment mean? | *I feel the same way as you | I want to share my sweets with you | I think you are wrong | I have never seen that film
exercise: «Public sentiment is against the new road.» What does public sentiment mean? | The price of petrol | A new road sign | A map of the town | *What many people feel
test: «There is no room for sentiment when you choose a team.» What does sentiment mean here? | A thought written on a card | The team's new uniform | *Tender feelings, such as love or pity, that get in the way | Running fast
test: Lily cried at every sad film. Her dad said she was very ... | silent | strict | sensible | *sentimental
exam: «Her grandmother wrote a lovely sentiment in the card.» What is a sentiment in this sentence? | A type of birthday cake | *A thought, expressed in words | A present for a friend | A loud party song
